						"value": true
					},
					"font-size": {
						"type": "float",
						"value": 19
					},
					"text": {
						"type": "string",
						"value": "SQUARE\nThe Square oscillator oscillates in a square waveshape. \nThis means that it jumps between its amplitude. \nAn amplitude of 1 means that the oscillation is between 1 and -1. \nThis is further affected by the offset, which is the amount that is added or subtracted from the output value.\n\nThe frequency dictates how fast the oscillator oscillates. \nPhase offset is a value that can be adjusted to offset the phase (start the oscillation at a different point in time). \n\nUnique to the Square oscillator is the addition of pulse width. \nAt 0.5 each pulse (positive and negative) has an equal length. \nWhen the pulse width is adjusted, either the positive or negative length will be extended and the other reduced."
					}
				},
				"bounds": {
					"height": 294.16839599609375,
					"width": 591.84912109375,
					"x": 58.4950408935546875,
